Z||000004||1slow60||12-25-2001||02:47 AM||||  They didnt say which sensors they were.  Like i said they wanted me to buy there book.  As far as I can find on my own the 3 conditions that must be met are<BR>1.  coolant temp at 105 degrees<BR>2.  Oxygen sensor up andrunning at temp  also something to do with the O2 cross count numbers but no specifics.<BR>3.  A specified amount of time that is built into the MEM CAL or PROM depending on the year of the computer.<BR>  All three of these conditions are supposed to be met in the first 30 seconds to 2 minutes of operation.  I know on mine I have had the coolant up to to 240 degrees today with crosscount #s as high as 211 and still no closed loop.  I hat ethe thought of it but the computer may be bad.  Anyone have experience with the auto zone remanufactured units? Or maybe know of a way to repair the board?  I am an electrician but not anything electronic really but I am willing to try if it saves me the price of that computer.  If it breaks it is still a core.  Thank guys||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroMaster88||12-25-2001||02:51 AM||||Take your computer apart and check the board for cracks.  The boards get old and brittle which causes them to break.  If that do that they may stop working, or work intermitentally.  I dont think it is your computer though.  Check all your wireing over so you are sure no wires are borken.  <P>You could try going to a salvage yard and purchasing a used computer.  May be cheaper.  Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000006||1slow60||12-25-2001||03:11 AM||||  Being that the computer is hanging down under the dash I think that i will go out and get it and take it apart and see what i got.  Right now the only bad wire that i know of in the harness, been chasing them for 3 weeks, is the one from the fuel pump relay to the fuel pump.  It energizes the the MAF power relay so I ran a jumper wire to the tan and white wire in another location from the bottom of that fuel pump relay till I get a chance to chase the wire around the fire wall.  All That did was bypass the bad part in the wire, not rework the wiring system.||||reg||1
